A metric showing how often a model's predictions of something being positive (or true) is actually correct.

How it is Used

Suppose the County uses a system to detect fraudulent claims in a social services program. If the system flags 10 claims as fraudulent, but only 1 is actually fraudulent, then the precision is 1/10 or 10%. Typically models strive for high precision, which means fewer false positives are predicted by the model (fewer claims that were wrongly flagged as fraudulent…";}s:8:"relation";a:2:{s:10:"references";a:2:{s:17:"glossary:accuracy";b:1;s:15:"glossary:recall";b:1;}s:10:"firstimage";s:0:"";}s:8:"internal";a:2:{s:5:"cache";b:1;s:3:"toc";b:1;}}s:10:"persistent";a:5:{s:4:"date";a:2:{s:7:"created";i:1744790717;s:8:"modified";i:1744791839;}s:7:"creator";s:11:"Aditya Seth";s:4:"user";s:5:"admin";s:11:"last_change";a:9:{s:4:"date";i:1744791839;s:2:"ip";s:14:"73.235.164.232";s:4:"type";s:1:"E";s:2:"id";s:18:"glossary:precision";s:4:"user";s:5:"admin";s:3:"sum";s:0:"";s:5:"extra";s:0:"";s:10:"sizechange";i:44;s:4:"mode";s:4:"page";}s:11:"contributor";a:1:{s:5:"admin";s:11:"Aditya Seth";}}}